    <element xsi:type="archimate:Outcome" name="Home Router Not Compromised" id="id-ab1b906764754f58930ac5bd06f0bc90">
      <documentation>While finding perfectly exact statistics on home routers is notoriously difficult due to the sheer variety of manufacturers and the private nature of home networks, several rigorous security studies, consumer advocacy audits, and threat landscape reports provide highly reliable estimates.&#xD;
The data clearly separates into two distinct realities: the vast majority of home routers are highly vulnerable, but only a fraction are actively compromised at any given moment—though that fraction still represents millions of active botnet nodes.&#xD;
&#xD;
------------------------------&#xD;
## B) Proportion of Home Routers Natively in a Compromised State&#xD;
While &quot;vulnerable&quot; means the front door is unlocked, &quot;compromised&quot; means someone has actively walked in. &#xD;
Obtaining an exact global percentage is challenging because compromised routers function silently as part of proxy networks or botnets. &#xD;
However, internet-wide scans and law enforcement takedowns give us a sense of scale: [6, 7] &#xD;
&#xD;
* &#xD;
* The 1% Global Baseline (Tens of Millions of Devices): Security researchers generally estimate that 1% to 2% of all globally deployed consumer routers are actively compromised and participating in botnets (like Mirai, Mozi, or state-sponsored networks) at any given moment. Given there are hundreds of millions of routers globally, this translates to several million compromised nodes.&#xD;
* The &quot;Lion's Share&quot; of IoT Malware: According to threat data, routers account for over 75% of all observed IoT cyberattacks and successful botnet recruitments. When threat actors target consumer smart environments, they bypass smart TVs or fridges and target the router because it offers total control over network traffic. [6, 8] &#xD;
* Mass Target Examples:&#xD;
* In massive targeted espionage and ransomware proxy campaigns (such as those run by groups like Volt Typhoon or the Russian GRU), networks of tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands of small-office/home-office (SOHO) routers are routinely discovered to be simultaneously compromised before being disrupted by federal agents.&#xD;
   * Forescout's Vedere Labs scanned specific vulnerable edge ecosystems and found that 38% of over 704,000 actively exposed routers remained fundamentally compromised or completely open to identical N-day exploits years after patches were made available. [7, 9, 10, 11] &#xD;
* &#xD;
&#xD;
## Why is the Gap Between &quot;Vulnerable&quot; and &quot;Compromised&quot; So Large?&#xD;
Hackers are opportunistic. While 80% of routers could be hacked, threat actors typically scan for specific, easily automated entry points—such as routers with remote management interfaces accidentally left open to the public internet, or devices using well-known, hard-coded factory passwords. If a router is vulnerable but hidden behind an ISP’s firewall without its remote management turned on, it is heavily shielded from automated internet-wide compromise attempts. [3, 5, 12] &#xD;
------------------------------&#xD;
Are you looking to evaluate the security posture of a specific router model, or are you compiling data to help secure remote workers' home environments?&#xD;
&#xD;
